You are given a program that already exists, and the changed tree that now
describes it. Make the smallest change to the program that the edit forces,
and no other change.

You are editing this program, not rewriting it. It is written. You are moving
one thing in it.

Every line the changed tree still implies survives, character for character,
including its indentation and the blank lines around it. Do not rename
anything, do not re-sign a function, do not reorder the definitions, do not
reformat, do not simplify, do not improve. If a line would have been written
the same way by a programmer who never saw the edit, that line stays exactly
as it is.

Change exactly what the changed tree now demands, and repair only what breaks
as a result: a call with no callee, a name with no binding. If the edit forces
nothing, return the program back unchanged, byte for byte.
